ivotal event in the European Theatre of World War II, marking the last major offensive by the Allies against the German capital. The battle began on April 16, 1945, and culminated in the surrender of German forces on May 2, 1945. The fighting was intense, with both sides suffering heavy losses. The battle's significance lies in its role as a turning point in the war, marking the beginning of the end of Nazi Germany's rule. The battle also had a profound impact on the city of Berlin, which was left in ruins. The battle's legacy can be seen in the formation of the post-war German government and the rebuilding of the city.

